#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f87bc14d-5e68-5a5e-bec8-f1b5b21c02e8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Arc Length of a Smooth Curve Using the Arc Length Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4dd42c4-9e1e-5eb7-a24b-f692baad9428" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292706} {:answer/id #uuid "2d1cf6b6-b159-5195-b5f1-20fb75d0909c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292707} {:answer/id #uuid "3e2a4a79-7a3d-55dc-bcf4-51cc36694ee4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292708} {:answer/id #uuid "2d75a79b-c2ab-54a7-a612-1d1ae36826c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292709} {:answer/id #uuid "f9de82a6-c58a-5cd8-93a6-d752714441c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d75a79b-c2ab-54a7-a612-1d1ae36826c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292709} :answer-field/id #uuid "246db429-8625-5142-b366-da5557858029" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4437946f-3374-5e77-9f64-df37a3956524" :question/math-academy-id "q-120752" :question/problem "Compute the arc length of $y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}$ for $x\\in [\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},1].$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}},$ $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and $b=1.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1-{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{1}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x){|}_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88a1b1e6-87cf-5f0e-89e3-cea5b1fadb89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{4x^{3}}{3} - 2x^{2} + x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082473} {:answer/id #uuid "ba201ae1-3784-5f25-b538-a3db1c7fd099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082474} {:answer/id #uuid "f883ee93-d216-5bdf-9ce7-a5943782e6b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x^{2} - 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082475} {:answer/id #uuid "f9187eac-d0d4-5321-8870-9cbe1376088d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x^{2} - x + 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082476} {:answer/id #uuid "74f5f5fe-3fd4-5fcb-81a6-e26c31bad4ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9187eac-d0d4-5321-8870-9cbe1376088d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x^{2} - x + 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082476} :answer-field/id #uuid "80edbeb1-18ba-5dd9-9b3f-f1d655ee4147" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab3de3f1-6311-5aac-86d4-b283aa079450" :question/math-academy-id "q-82624" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2+4{x}^{2}-4x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2+4{x}^{2}-4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=4{x}^{2}-4x+1=(2x-1)^{2}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm (2x-1).\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int (2x-1)\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm ({x}^{2}-x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y={x}^{2}-x+4.$"}]}
